*   Topic: Home energy dashboards: identifying an accessibility blind spot.
    *   Format: Substack Note.
    *   Length: 60-100 words.
    *   Structure: plain text, one short paragraph or two.
    *   Constraints: No hashtags, no title.

    *   Home energy dashboards are great for tracking usage.
    *   However, they often ignore accessibility (color blindness, screen reader compatibility, cognitive load/complexity).
    *   If the data isn't accessible, we aren't truly helping everyone de-carbonize or save money.

    We're obsessed with home energy dashboards to slash carbon footprints, but we're missing a massive blind spot: accessibility. Most of these interfaces rely heavily on complex color-coded graphs and dense data visualizations that are impossible for people with visual impairments or cognitive disabilities to navigate. If we want a truly inclusive energy transition, our tools need to move beyond just flashy aesthetics. A dashboard that isn't accessible to everyone isn't just flawed—it leaves people behind.

    *   *Word count check:* 88 words.
    *   *Format:* Plain text, one paragraph.
    *   *Constraints:* No hashtags, no title.
